<!--Post: 992--><!--Time: 1094373409-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST992"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 05, 2004 - 1:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->From the <i>Wichita Eagle</i>, 9-5-2004: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/9586542.htm" target="_blank">Death penalty unlikely for BTK</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 992-->
<!--Post: 993--><!--Time: 1094406396-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST993"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 05, 2004 - 10:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The State of Kansas has no balls if they cannot execute BTK. If he killed Jacob Allen, he can be executed under the media&#39;s theory. Thank God I live in Texas where we carry out the death penalty. If BTK can execute others without due process, he can be executed with due process. Should he be caught, he will make that a game as well. <BR> <BR>If he is not material for the death penality, I cannot imagine who would be. Failure to give him the death penalty will make fools of the victims, their survivors, citizens, and law enforcement.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 993-->
<!--Post: 995--><!--Time: 1094421773-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST995"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 05, 2004 - 3:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If BTK was convicted of a murder that took place after the death penalty went into effect in 1994, he would not be immediately executed. Wouldn&#39;t there be at least one automatic appeal? It might take 8-15 years of due process if his lawyer&#39;s tactics and maneuvers delayed everything to the benefit of his killer client. The families of the other victims would want their day in court against him. The cold case victims&#39; families would want to keep him alive to see if he might confess to those murders. BTK, whether captured or surrendered, would probably die of old age.  <BR>-Deb<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 995-->
<!--Post: 999--><!--Time: 1094458120-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST999"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, September 06, 2004 - 1:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, if someone sentenced to death <i>before</i> the death penalty was overturned can have his sentence commuted to life in prison &#40;such as Charles Manson&#41;, why then can&#39;t someone who committed several murders while the death penalty was overturned be executed if sentenced to death <i>after</i> it&#39;s reinstated?  Why does it work one way, and not the other???<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 999-->
<!--Post: 1000--><!--Time: 1094479571-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1000"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, September 06, 2004 - 7:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Amen, Ed!!! I agree wholeheartedly. Penal codes also define deadly weapons. If venetian blind cording is not listed as a deadly weapon, I guess that he did not kill anyone! When will this insanity stop. The Warren Court did this to the US, they are dead, and we live with the repercussions.  Victims should get at least equal consideration under the law and that is not the case. <BR> <BR>My time spent as a probation officer indicated that the majority of people on probation did not truly &#39;commit a crime&#39;. It was their life style. That was the way they thought, lived, and went about their business. And it will be with great difficulty that life styles are changed. Personal integrity was just not a concept they possessed. They cannot be helped;they can only be herded. I do not say that discompassionately. As a psychologist, it was difficult to look past the person and the pain they had created for themselves. Yet, the criminal justice system holds the &#39;body&#39; accountable for deeds done in the body, regardless of the condition of the heart and spirit at the time of the execution. &#40;I had certification as a Texas Peace officer as well.&#41; <BR> <BR>We are diminished when someone dies and I personally experience a sense of failure when I hear of an execution, but I still staunchly support the death penalty. It does prevent that person from committing other crimes and prevents them from further reproduction. And I won&#39;t go off into conjugal visits. <BR> <BR>Should BTK ever come within the reach of the criminal justice system, the judge will hopefully rule that he cannot profit from his story, at least.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1000-->
